From: aa-proxy <noreply@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] Bluetooth: skip HCI_OP_WRITE_DEF_LINK_POLICY entirely for sprd

On top of 0001-bluetooth-fix-sprd-broken-park-link-status.patch (Paolo
Sabatino's upstream quirk, also shipped in Orange Pi's vendor kernel):
that patch only strips the HCI_LP_PARK bit from the
HCI_OP_WRITE_DEF_LINK_POLICY payload before sending it, on the
assumption the chip only mishandles "park" specifically.

On our AW859A/UWE5622 (firmware MARLIN3_19B_W21.05.3 /
sc2355_marlin3_lite_ott), the command still fails
("Opcode 0x080f failed: -22" / "Can't init device: Invalid argument")
regardless of the policy bits requested - confirmed on both mainline
and Orange Pi's own vendor kernel, which carries the same upstream
quirk and hits the same failure. Skip sending the command entirely
for devices with the quirk set instead of only adjusting its payload.

--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c
@@ -4109,6 +4109,14 @@
 	if (!(hdev->commands[5] & 0x10))
 		return 0;

+	/* Some sprd/uwe5622 (ttyBT) firmware revisions reject
+	 * HCI_OP_WRITE_DEF_LINK_POLICY outright, regardless of the policy
+	 * bits requested, not just when HCI_LP_PARK is set. Skip sending
+	 * the command entirely for those.
+	 */
+	if (test_bit(HCI_QUIRK_BROKEN_PARK_LINK_STATUS, &hdev->quirks))
+		return 0;
+
 	memset(&cp, 0, sizeof(cp));

 	if (lmp_rswitch_capable(hdev))
@@ -4117,7 +4125,7 @@
 		link_policy |= HCI_LP_HOLD;
 	if (lmp_sniff_capable(hdev))
 		link_policy |= HCI_LP_SNIFF;
-	if (lmp_park_capable(hdev) && !test_bit(HCI_QUIRK_BROKEN_PARK_LINK_STATUS, &hdev->quirks))
+	if (lmp_park_capable(hdev))
 		link_policy |= HCI_LP_PARK;

 	cp.policy = cpu_to_le16(link_policy);
